*(
    title: Geometric Proof with Vectors
    description: Turning claims about a diagram into claims about vectors, where they can be computed instead of seen.
    tags: vectors, geometric proof, midpoints, parallelogram, centroid, dot product, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    A classical geometric proof depends on the diagram being drawn correctly and on the reader seeing the relevant configuration. A vector proof depends on neither. Choose an origin, name each point by its position vector, and a statement about the figure becomes an identity to be verified.

    definition{
        The vector from a chosen origin $O$ to the point $A$, written $\mathbf{a}$ or $\overrightarrow{OA}$. Once every point has one, the vector between any two points is a difference: $\overrightarrow{AB} = \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}$.
    }(
        term: Position vector
    )

    ===

    The first result is the one everything else leans on. The midpoint $M$ of $AB$ is reached by going to $A$ and then half way along $\overrightarrow{AB}$, so $\mathbf{m} = \mathbf{a} + \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$.

    equation{
        \mathbf{m} = \frac{\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}}{2}
    }

    On the canvas $\mathbf{a} = (4, 1)$ and $\mathbf{b} = (1, 3)$, and the midpoint is $(2.5, 2)$. The formula is symmetric in $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$, which it must be, since going from $B$ half way to $A$ arrives at the same place.

    A point dividing $AB$ in some other ratio is handled the same way: $\mathbf{a} + t(\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a})$ is the point $t$ of the way from $A$ to $B$, and $t = \frac{1}{2}$ is the midpoint.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 4; dy: 1; colour: orange; label: $\mathbf{a}$)
        vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 1; dy: 3; colour: purple; label: $\mathbf{b}$)
        segment(start: (4, 1); end: (1, 3); colour: grey; dashed)

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 2.5; dy: 2; colour: teal; label: $\mathbf{m} = \tfrac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$)
            point(x: 2.5; y: 2; colour: teal; label: $M$)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-1, 5]
        y domain: [-1, 4]
    )
]

scene{
    # The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other

    Take the origin at one vertex $O$, with $\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}$. The fourth vertex $C$ of the parallelogram $OACB$ is reached by going along $\mathbf{a}$ and then along $\mathbf{b}$, so $\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$.

    ===

    The diagonal $OC$ has midpoint $\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{c} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$. The other diagonal $AB$ has midpoint $\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$ by the formula of the first scene. The two midpoints are the same vector, so they are the same point, and each diagonal passes through the middle of the other.

    equation{
        \text{midpoint of } OC = \frac{\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}}{2} = \text{midpoint of } AB
    }

    The proof is two lines, and neither of them is about the picture. It never used that the sides were parallel except through the single fact $\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$, which is what makes $OACB$ a parallelogram in the first place.

    note{
        The converse holds too. If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, the shared midpoint gives $\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{c} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$, so $\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$ and the figure is a parallelogram. Vector arguments often run in both directions with no extra work.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        polygon(x: 0, 4, 5, 1; y: 0, 1, 4, 3; colour: teal; !fill)
        point(x: 4; y: 1; colour: orange; label: $A$)
        point(x: 1; y: 3; colour: purple; label: $B$)
        point(x: 5; y: 4; colour: grey; label: $C$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (0, 0); end: (5, 4); colour: orange; label: $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$)
            segment(start: (4, 1); end: (1, 3); colour: purple)
            point(x: 2.5; y: 2; colour: teal; label: $\tfrac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-1, 6]
        y domain: [-1, 5]
    )
]

scene{
    # The medians of a triangle are concurrent

    A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side. A triangle has three of them, and the claim is that all three pass through one point.

    The vector proof names the point first and then checks it. Let the vertices have position vectors $\mathbf{a}$, $\mathbf{b}$ and $\mathbf{c}$, and consider $\mathbf{g} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c})$.

    ===

    The median from $A$ runs to the midpoint $\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c})$ of $BC$. Two thirds of the way along it is $\mathbf{a} + \frac{2}{3}\left(\frac{\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}}{2} - \mathbf{a}\right)$, which simplifies to $\mathbf{g}$.

    equation{
        \mathbf{a} + \frac{2}{3}\left(\frac{\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}}{2} - \mathbf{a}\right) = \frac{\mathbf{a}}{3} + \frac{\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}}{3} = \frac{\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}}{3}
    }

    ===

    The expression for $\mathbf{g}$ is symmetric in the three vertices, so the same calculation starting from $B$ or from $C$ lands on the same point. All three medians pass through $\mathbf{g}$, each at two thirds of its length from the vertex.

    definition{
        The point $\frac{1}{3}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c})$ at which the three medians of a triangle meet, two thirds of the way along each from its vertex.
    }(
        term: Centroid
    )

    The symmetry did the work that a classical proof does with similar triangles. Once the candidate point is written down in a form that treats the vertices identically, checking one median checks them all.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        polygon(x: 0, 6, 2; y: 0, 0, 5; colour: grey; !fill)
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $A$)
        point(x: 6; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $B$)
        point(x: 2; y: 5; colour: orange; label: $C$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (0, 0); end: (4, 2.5); colour: teal)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1s
        )

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (6, 0); end: (1, 2.5); colour: teal)
            segment(start: (2, 5); end: (3, 0); colour: teal)
            point(x: 2.667; y: 1.667; colour: purple; label: $G$)
        }(
            in: 2
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-1, 7]
        y domain: [-1, 6]
    )
]

scene{
    ## A right angle from a dot product of zero

    The angle in a semicircle is a right angle: join the ends of a diameter to any point on the circle, and the two chords meet at $90^\circ$. The classical proof draws in the radius to the point and chases angles in two isosceles triangles. The vector proof computes one dot product.

    Put the origin at the centre, with radius $r$. The ends of the diameter are $\mathbf{a}$ and $-\mathbf{a}$, and the point $P$ on the circle is $\mathbf{p}$, so $|\mathbf{a}| = |\mathbf{p}| = r$.

    ===

    The two chords from $P$ are $\overrightarrow{PA} = \mathbf{a} - \mathbf{p}$ and $\overrightarrow{PB} = -\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{p}$. Their dot product expands like a difference of two squares.

    equation{
        (\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{p}) \cdot (-\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{p}) = -\left(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a} - \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{p}\right) = -\left(r^2 - r^2\right) = 0
    }

    The chords are perpendicular, and the argument never looked at where $P$ was. That is the strength of the method: a single calculation covers every position of $P$ at once, where a diagram covers one.

    task{
        Prove that the segment joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half its length.
    }[
        With vertices $\mathbf{a}$, $\mathbf{b}$, $\mathbf{c}$, the midpoints of $AB$ and $AC$ are $\mathbf{m} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})$ and $\mathbf{n} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c})$. Then $\overrightarrow{MN} = \mathbf{n} - \mathbf{m} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{b}) = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{BC}$, which is parallel to $\overrightarrow{BC}$ and half its length.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )
}[
    canvas.graph{
        circle(x: 0; y: 0; size: 2; colour: grey; !fill)
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        segment(start: (-2, 0); end: (2, 0); colour: grey)
        point(x: 2; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $A$)
        point(x: -2; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $B$)
        point(x: 1.2; y: 1.6; colour: purple; label: $P$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (1.2, 1.6); end: (2, 0); colour: teal)
            segment(start: (1.2, 1.6); end: (-2, 0); colour: teal)
            angle(at: (1.2, 1.6); from: (2, 0); to: (-2, 0); colour: teal; radius: 22)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-3, 3]
        y domain: [-2.5, 2.5]
        !show ticks
    )
]
